Request success ratio|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status!~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[5m]))|A PromQL expression computes a short-window request success ratio.|Compare a defined checkout population with its availability objective.|Result 0.999 means 99.9% success under this definition. No traffic can produce no usable ratio; status labels and eligibility must match instrumentation.|0.999|Illustrative PromQL, not a shell command. Assumes a counter named http_requests_total and defines only 5xx as bad; adapt to actual user failures.
Error budget burn|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[5m])) / 0.001|Computes burn rate for a 99.9% event-based objective.|Translate current failure rate into budget consumption speed.|1 is the sustainable rate; 10 means ten times that rate. Zero traffic needs explicit handling.|10|Illustrative PromQL; use the same eligible population as the SLO and pair short and long windows.
Long-window bad ratio|sum(increase(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status=~"5.."}[30d])) / sum(increase(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[30d]))|Estimates the bad-event fraction over thirty days.|Review accumulated budget use rather than only current health.|Compare with 0.001 for a 99.9% target; telemetry gaps and counter retention limit accuracy.|0.0008|Illustrative PromQL; long queries may be expensive and require sufficient retention or recording rules.
Tail latency|histogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket{job="checkout"}[5m])))|Estimates p99 from a classic Prometheus latency histogram.|Check whether slow-tail behavior changed during a rollout.|The result is seconds; bucket boundaries determine estimation accuracy. It is not an average of instance p99 values.|0.42|Illustrative PromQL; metric and labels must exist, and sparse traffic makes estimates unstable.
Traffic rate|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[5m]))|Calculates total requests per second for a service.|Distinguish a traffic surge from a capacity regression.|rate handles counter resets per series before sum combines instances.|1250|Illustrative PromQL; retries may be counted as requests, so define the population clearly.
Queue age|max(queue_oldest_message_age_seconds{queue="orders"})|Selects the largest reported age of pending work.|Detect processing delay that a queue-length graph can conceal.|Seconds above the delivery objective indicate work waiting too long, not necessarily a failed worker.|180|Illustrative application metric; confirm whether empty queues report zero or no series.
Dependency latency probe|curl -sS -o /dev/null --max-time 5 -w 'status=%{http_code} total=%{time_total}\n' https://SERVICE/health|Makes one bounded HTTP health request and reports status and total time.|Compare reachability from a specific incident location.|A 200 from a shallow health endpoint does not prove the business transaction works.|status=200 total=0.080|Replace SERVICE with an authorized safe endpoint; do not load-test production with an uncontrolled loop.
Deployment progress|kubectl rollout status deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE --timeout=60s|Observes progress for a Kubernetes deployment.|Check whether a recent change has completed before interpreting its health.|A success message is controller progress; compare error and latency SLIs before declaring recovery.|deployment successfully rolled out|Kubernetes-backed example; read-only and requires the intended context.
Recent service evidence|journalctl -u APP.service --since '-15 min' --no-pager|Reads recent service logs on a systemd host.|Build a bounded incident timeline without dumping all history.|Correlate timestamps with deployment and alert times; missing logs may reflect retention.|APP: dependency timeout ...|Host-based example; read-only and output may contain sensitive data.
Host saturation snapshot|vmstat 1 5|Samples host scheduling, paging, I/O and CPU activity.|Check whether a service slowdown correlates with resource pressure.|Ignore the first since-boot average when comparing short intervals; inspect runnable tasks, swap and wait together.|r b ... si so ... us sy id wa|Linux example; a snapshot cannot establish the full incident history.
Replica readiness|kubectl get deployment APP -n NAMESPACE|Summarizes desired and available application replicas.|Check whether traffic is being served with reduced capacity.|READY compares ready with desired replicas; AVAILABLE reflects availability conditions, not end-user success.|NAME READY UP-TO-DATE AVAILABLE AGE|Kubernetes-backed example; read-only and dependent on controller observations.
Recent change history|git log --since='2 hours ago' --oneline --all|Lists locally available commits in a recent window.|Generate candidate changes to correlate with an incident.|Commit timestamps do not prove deployment time; map revisions to deployment records.|abc123 Adjust connection pool|Read-only; fetch deployment metadata separately and avoid assuming correlation proves cause.
Long-window burn rate|sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status=~"5.."}[1h])) / sum(rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[1h])) / 0.001|Compares the one-hour error ratio with the allowed ratio for a 99.9% SLO.|Pair a sustained failure signal with a shorter window before paging.|A value of 6 means six times the budget-compatible error rate, not 6% errors. A zero denominator requires a no-traffic policy.|6|PromQL; assumes 5xx defines bad eligible events and the metric has enough samples. Validate the SLI definition.
Regional success ratio|sum by (region) (rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status!~"5.."}[5m])) / sum by (region) (rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[5m]))|Calculates success fraction separately by region.|Find a failing region hidden by a healthy global average.|Each output value belongs to one region; 0.98 means a 98% success ratio for that region's eligible traffic.|{region="eu"} 0.98|PromQL with matching region labels; choose the same eligibility and success definitions in numerator and denominator.
Canary error comparison|sum by (version) (rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout",status=~"5.."}[5m])) / sum by (version) (rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[5m]))|Compares recent error ratios grouped by deployed version.|Assess whether a canary differs from the baseline.|Compare ratios together with sample size and traffic mix; a low-volume version may have noisy estimates.|{version="canary"} 0.04|PromQL; version labels and representative traffic are prerequisites. This query alone does not establish causation.
Traffic by region|sum by (region) (rate(http_requests_total{job="checkout"}[5m]))|Calculates request rate per region.|Check load movement during failover or traffic restoration.|Values are requests per second for each region; compare them with safe surviving capacity.|{region="eu"} 1200|PromQL using an illustrative request counter; verify region labels and traffic eligibility.
Unavailable scrape targets|sum by (job) (up == bool 0)|Counts discovered scrape targets currently reporting a failed scrape.|Check the breadth of a telemetry collection incident.|A value of 2 means two selected targets have up=0; targets absent from discovery are not counted.|{job="api"} 2|PromQL; this measures scraping, not direct customer availability. Add discovery/absence coverage separately.
Backlog peak during recovery|max_over_time(queue_depth{queue="orders"}[15m])|Reports the largest sampled queue depth during the recent recovery window.|Check whether restoring traffic creates a larger backlog than before.|The value is messages at the sampled peak; it does not measure oldest message age or processing throughput.|{queue="orders"} 1800|PromQL with an example gauge; compare with queue age and worker processing rate.
Bounded endpoint sample|curl -sS --max-time 5 -D - -o /dev/null https://SERVICE/health|Requests a health endpoint and prints response headers without the body.|Collect status and routing evidence during a bounded incident check.|The HTTP status and selected headers show this one response; a 200 on a shallow health endpoint may not represent critical journeys.|HTTP/2 503|Sends one request; use an authorized endpoint and avoid exposing authentication headers in collected evidence.
Deployment revision evidence|kubectl rollout history deployment/APP -n NAMESPACE|Lists retained revisions of an affected Kubernetes workload.|Correlate release timing and recovery candidates during triage.|Revision numbers identify controller history, not necessarily source commits or database migration compatibility.|REVISION CHANGE-CAUSE|Read-only; confirm context and namespace before proposing rollback.
Current deployment failures|kubectl get pods -n NAMESPACE --field-selector=status.phase!=Running -o wide|Lists Pods outside the Running phase in one namespace.|Find Pending or failed workload instances during response.|STATUS and NODE help separate scheduling from startup issues; Running Pods with failing readiness are not included by this filter.|api-... 0/1 Pending ...|Read-only; complement with readiness checks because phase is not a full health model.
Recent host boot|uptime -s|Prints the host's boot time.|Check whether a host restart aligns with an incident timeline.|The timestamp is in the host's local time zone; correlate using a consistent time basis.|2026-10-23 09:42:00|Read-only Linux procps command; boot time is not application deployment time.
Changed release scope|git diff --name-status LAST_GOOD CURRENT|Lists paths changed between two verified release revisions.|Scope a release-related incident before reading the relevant patches.|A, M and D mark added, modified and deleted paths; a filename change alone does not prove causation.|M src/checkout.js|Read-only; replace refs with deployed commit identities, not an assumed branch tip.
Backup archive integrity|gzip -t backup.sql.gz|Tests the gzip stream's integrity without extracting it.|Catch a corrupt or incomplete compressed backup before a restore exercise.|No output with exit 0 means the compressed stream passes checks; it does not establish database consistency or recoverability.|No output on successful integrity test|Read-only but reads the full archive; follow with an isolated application-level restore and verification.
